This requires that the referees recognise and judge passive methods in a consistent manner throughout
the game.

Situations
Passive play may arise in all phases of a team’s attack, including for example:
- when the ball is moved down the court slowly;
- during the first positional attack (i.e. normal build-up phase);
- after unsuccessful attacking actions or attacking actions interrupted by a free throw (i.e. shorter
build-up phase);
- after interruptions of the game (e.g. team time-out).
Passive play is typically used more frequently in the following situations:
- the score is close near the end of a game;
- a team has to play short-handed (five against six; four against six; others);
- empty goal situations: the attacking team plays without goalkeeper (seven against six; six
against six; others);
- active defending destroys/interrupts the pace of the attack and prevents a targeted change of
pace (see detailed criteria);
- different performance levels of the two teams (general superiority of one team, especially in
defence);
- unsuccessful attacking actions by one team for a longer period of time.

